(CHAPTER 4: VISUAL ANALYTICS & EXECUTIVE REPORTING \(Page 4\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Chapter 4: Visual Analytics · Section 4.1 · Page 4]) Tj T*
(Visual encoding maps data attributes to perceptual graphical properties \(position,) Tj T*
(length, angle, color saturation, shape\).) Tj T*
(Research by Cleveland and McGill establishes that human visual perception decodes) Tj T*
(position along a common scale with the highest accuracy, followed by length, angle,) Tj T*
(area, and color saturation:) Tj T*
(- Bar Charts: Superior for comparing discrete categorical magnitudes along a common) Tj T*
(baseline.) Tj T*
(- Line Charts: Optimal for displaying continuous temporal trends.) Tj T*
(- Scatter Plots: Best for discovering correlation, clustering, and outlier) Tj T*
(distributions between two continuous variables.) Tj T*
(- Avoid 3D charts, pie charts with more than 4 slices \(since angles and areas are) Tj T*
(difficult for human eyes to compare accurately\), and dual-axis charts with deceptive) Tj T*
(mismatched scale ranges.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Chapter 4: Visual Analytics · Section 4.2 · Page 4]) Tj T*
(Dashboard Architecture and Diagnostic Flow:) Tj T*
(An effective executive dashboard follows the Visual Information-Seeking Mantra \(Ben) Tj T*
(Shneiderman\): "Overview first, zoom and filter, then details-on-demand.") Tj T*
(1. Top Tier: Strategic high-level KPI summary cards displaying current metric, target) Tj T*
(variance, and direction trend indicators.) Tj T*
(2. Middle Tier: Interactive comparative visuals \(e.g., regional breakdown, monthly) Tj T*
(trend\) allowing users to filter by segment.) Tj T*
(3. Bottom Tier: Granular drill-down tables providing exact transactional details when) Tj T*
(requested.) Tj T*
(Actionable dashboards provide diagnosis rather than mere descriptive metrics,) Tj T*
(explicitly answering 'Why did this metric change?' and guiding operational) Tj T*
(interventions.) Tj T*
ET
